  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "A normal duck has two legs. A lame duck has one leg. A sitting duck has no legs. There are 33 ducks with 32 legs. The total number of normal ducks and lame ducks is twice the number of sitting ducks. How many lame ducks are there?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "12 lame ducks.",
      "worked": "Let $n$, $l$ and $s$ be the numbers of normal, lame and sitting ducks, respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}n+l+s&=33 &&(1)\\\\2n+l&=32 &&(2)\\\\n+l&=2s &&(3)\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute (3) into (1):\n$$\\begin{align*}2s+s&=33\\\\3s&=33\\\\s&=11\\\\n+l&=22\\end{align*}$$\nSubtract $n+l=22$ from (2), then substitute:\n$$\\begin{align*}n&=10\\\\10+l&=22\\\\l&=12\\end{align*}$$\nThere are 12 lame ducks.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 116
  },
